WEBVTT

00:04.000 --> 00:04.520
Okay, guys.

00:04.520 --> 00:08.720
So now we're gonna go and understand the rules or let's say the limitations of window functions.

00:08.720 --> 00:13.480
So let's learn what you are not allowed to do while using window functions okay.

00:13.560 --> 00:20.080
So the first rule that you are allowed to use the window function only in the Select clause and as well

00:20.080 --> 00:21.360
in the order by clause.

00:21.360 --> 00:25.680
So here we have again the same example where we're finding the total sales by the order status.

00:25.680 --> 00:29.160
So as you can see we used the window function in the Select clause.

00:29.200 --> 00:31.240
And we didn't get any error right.

00:31.240 --> 00:34.440
So now we can go and use it as well in the order by.

00:34.480 --> 00:40.160
So let's say order by and let's go and copy everything but not the name in the order by.

00:40.200 --> 00:44.360
So if I go and execute this there will be no errors and SQL going to allow it.

00:44.360 --> 00:46.120
And as you can see the result didn't change.

00:46.120 --> 00:48.840
So let's go and sort it for example descending.

00:48.840 --> 00:50.760
So I'm going to write here descending.

00:50.760 --> 00:52.040
And let's execute.

00:52.080 --> 00:55.920
Now we have the total sales with the highest values then the lowest values.

00:55.920 --> 01:01.200
So having this rule that we can use it only in select and order by that means we cannot use window functions

01:01.200 --> 01:02.800
in order to filter data.

01:03.120 --> 01:06.990
So let me show you for example instead of order by.

01:07.030 --> 01:11.910
Let's have a where clause where the total sales, let's say bigger than 100.

01:11.910 --> 01:13.470
So let's go and execute this.

01:13.470 --> 01:16.670
And as you can see SQL going to say no you are not allowed to do that.

01:16.670 --> 01:19.110
You can do that only for select and order by.

01:19.150 --> 01:23.110
So we are not allowed to use it for filtering data using the Where clause.

01:23.270 --> 01:25.710
And as well you are not allowed to use it in the group by.

01:25.950 --> 01:30.310
So if I go and do a group by and I will remove the condition over here.

01:30.630 --> 01:32.750
So if you execute it, you're going to get the same error.

01:32.790 --> 01:38.950
You are not allowed to use the window function in the group by so only with the order by or as well

01:38.950 --> 01:40.150
in the Select clause.

01:40.190 --> 01:41.910
Okay, so now to the second rule.

01:41.950 --> 01:45.910
You cannot use window functions inside another window function.

01:45.910 --> 01:49.430
So that means you cannot go and nest window functions together.

01:49.470 --> 01:50.830
Let me show you what I mean with that.

01:50.830 --> 01:52.190
So let's remove the group.

01:52.190 --> 01:54.510
By now everything should be working.

01:54.790 --> 01:59.030
Let's take and copy the whole window function over here and let's just nest it.

01:59.030 --> 02:04.230
So instead of sales we're going to have now window function inside another window function.

02:04.230 --> 02:07.830
So as you can see this is the inner window function.

02:08.030 --> 02:11.390
And the rest the outside is the outside of the function.

02:11.390 --> 02:16.780
So if I go and execute this, you will see that SQL can tell us you cannot use the window function in

02:16.780 --> 02:19.100
the context of another window function.

02:19.100 --> 02:22.340
So we cannot do nesting using window functions.

02:22.340 --> 02:25.540
So as you can see this is another limitation for those functions.

02:25.580 --> 02:27.100
All right moving to the third rule.

02:27.100 --> 02:28.180
Let's say an info.

02:28.740 --> 02:33.060
The window function can be executed after filtering the data with the where clause.

02:33.100 --> 02:34.180
Let's have an example.

02:34.180 --> 02:37.900
So okay so now let's say that I would like to have the same information as the total sales for each

02:37.900 --> 02:42.100
status, but only for two products 101 and 102.

02:42.140 --> 02:43.740
So let's go and do that.

02:43.740 --> 02:45.660
So we're going to use the Where clause.

02:45.860 --> 02:47.940
And then we're going to say product ID.

02:47.940 --> 02:51.500
And we're going to specify 101 and 102.

02:51.540 --> 02:53.620
So let's go and execute this.

02:53.620 --> 02:55.740
Now you can see we still have two partitions.

02:55.740 --> 02:58.060
So one for the delivered and one for the shipped.

02:58.300 --> 03:04.060
But the total sales is reduced because we are only focusing on two products and we filtered the whole

03:04.060 --> 03:04.700
data sets.

03:04.740 --> 03:06.060
So how SQL works.

03:06.060 --> 03:08.540
First the Where clause is going to be executed.

03:08.540 --> 03:11.620
And then the window function is going to be calculated.

03:11.620 --> 03:14.260
So that means first filtering and then aggregations.

03:14.300 --> 03:14.700
Okay guys.

03:14.700 --> 03:18.850
Now we're going to move to the last rule to the most interesting one and it says the following.

03:19.210 --> 03:24.490
You are allowed to use the window function together with a group by clause only if you use the same

03:24.490 --> 03:25.170
columns.

03:25.170 --> 03:25.810
So let me explain.

03:25.810 --> 03:26.330
What do I mean?

03:26.330 --> 03:27.530
But first some coffee.

03:30.930 --> 03:36.810
Let's have the following task and it says rank the customers based on their total sales.

03:36.810 --> 03:41.250
So now it sounds really easy, but if you check it, you have here two calculations.

03:41.370 --> 03:43.890
The first one you have to rank the customers.

03:43.890 --> 03:46.170
And the second calculation is an aggregation.

03:46.370 --> 03:49.170
You have to find the total sales for each customers.

03:49.210 --> 03:49.370
Okay.

03:49.410 --> 03:52.730
So now I'm going to show you step by step how I usually solve those tasks.

03:52.930 --> 03:55.050
So for now let's check the total sales.

03:55.410 --> 03:56.650
It is an aggregation right.

03:56.650 --> 03:58.530
So we can use the sum function.

03:58.530 --> 04:03.170
And this function is available in both groupby and as well in the window function.

04:03.450 --> 04:05.130
So for now I'm going to go with the groupby.

04:05.130 --> 04:07.410
And that's because the task is very simple.

04:07.410 --> 04:09.530
We don't have to show any other details right.

04:09.730 --> 04:11.330
So it's all about aggregations.

04:11.530 --> 04:13.170
So why not using the groupby.

04:13.290 --> 04:16.610
And now to the first part where we have to rank the customers.

04:16.610 --> 04:19.770
We cannot use the rank function with a groupby, right?

04:19.810 --> 04:21.850
GroupBy uses only aggregations.

04:21.850 --> 04:24.550
So here we are forced to use the window function.

04:24.550 --> 04:29.230
So that means for the rank I'm going to use window function for the total sales I'm going to use a groupie.

04:29.270 --> 04:31.190
So now let's do it step by step.

04:31.310 --> 04:35.550
So first we have to find the total sales for each customer using groupie.

04:35.590 --> 04:36.550
It's very simple.

04:36.790 --> 04:40.230
So I'm just going to remove all those stuff in our select statements.

04:40.390 --> 04:45.550
We need the customer ID and then we don't need a window function over here.

04:45.550 --> 04:49.790
And then after the from we're going to have a group by customer ID.

04:49.790 --> 04:53.870
So now I'm just grouping the customers and finding the sum of all sales.

04:53.910 --> 04:55.470
Let's go and execute this.

04:55.470 --> 04:58.110
So now as you can see in the results we have four customers.

04:58.110 --> 04:59.830
And that's why we have four rows.

04:59.870 --> 05:01.670
And as well we have the total sales.

05:01.670 --> 05:05.750
So let's say the half of the tasks is already solved right now.

05:05.750 --> 05:06.550
What is missing.

05:06.550 --> 05:08.470
Thus we need a rank.

05:08.470 --> 05:09.910
So let's go and build that.

05:10.350 --> 05:13.270
The second step we're going to use the rank function.

05:13.270 --> 05:14.910
And we're going to define a window for that.

05:14.910 --> 05:20.310
So over and inside it will not partition the data at all because it's already like grouped up.

05:20.310 --> 05:24.550
So what we're going to do over order by the rank function always needs an order by.

05:24.590 --> 05:25.150
Don't worry about it.

05:25.150 --> 05:26.150
We can talk about it later.

05:26.150 --> 05:29.460
So now we are ranking the data based on the total sales.

05:29.460 --> 05:31.420
That means the sum of sales.

05:31.420 --> 05:35.900
So what we're going to do, let's just go and copy this and put it after the order by.

05:35.940 --> 05:38.980
And now we have to decide whether ascending or descending is going to be descending.

05:38.980 --> 05:41.380
So the highest sales first and then the lowest sales.

05:41.380 --> 05:44.740
So now as you can see we have now a rank customers.

05:45.060 --> 05:49.020
And we have a window function now together with the groupby.

05:49.060 --> 05:52.460
So now let's go and execute this and see whether SQL can allow it.

05:52.620 --> 05:53.540
So let's run it.

05:53.540 --> 05:55.660
And as you can see it runs it.

05:55.660 --> 05:58.100
And we will get the rank for each customers.

05:58.340 --> 06:01.340
So the customer three has the highest total sale.

06:01.580 --> 06:02.780
Then the customer number one.

06:02.780 --> 06:06.500
And the last one going to be customer number two with the lowest total sales.

06:06.500 --> 06:06.900
All right.

06:06.900 --> 06:08.100
So we solved the tasks.

06:08.100 --> 06:11.740
We have now ranked the customers based on their total sales.

06:11.740 --> 06:17.260
So as you can see SQL allows you to use window function together with the groupby but only with one

06:17.260 --> 06:17.620
rule.

06:17.620 --> 06:22.580
Anything that you are using inside the window function should be part of the groupby.

06:22.740 --> 06:27.460
So for example, we fulfill the rule because we are using the sum of sales and the sum of sales is a

06:27.460 --> 06:28.980
part of the groupby.

06:28.980 --> 06:29.340
Right?

06:29.340 --> 06:33.770
So now if I go and just break the rule by not using the sum, just using the cells.

06:34.090 --> 06:40.690
So if I just remove the sum and use only the cells, SQL will not allow it because the cells is not

06:40.690 --> 06:41.730
part of the groupby.

06:41.770 --> 06:43.690
So as you can see, SQL is very strict with this.

06:43.730 --> 06:49.890
If you want to use everything in one query without using subqueries and so on, you have to use exact

06:49.890 --> 06:50.690
same columns.

06:50.770 --> 06:54.530
So for example, if I go over here instead of sales, I use the customer ID.

06:55.130 --> 06:59.450
So since the customer ID is a part of the group by SQL allows it.

06:59.450 --> 07:02.290
So be careful using window function together with the groupby.

07:02.330 --> 07:07.410
As long as you are using the same columns, nothing can go wrong and SQL allows it.

07:07.450 --> 07:11.970
Okay, so now I'm just going to go and fix this and let's run it.

07:12.010 --> 07:14.770
So now as you can see it's really easy if you follow those steps.

07:15.090 --> 07:17.170
First build the query using groupby.

07:17.210 --> 07:18.850
So don't you think about the window function.

07:18.850 --> 07:24.530
Just build the groupby and then the next step, the last one you go and define and build the window

07:24.530 --> 07:25.050
function.

07:25.050 --> 07:30.250
So with that you can solve really nice analytical use cases with a simple one query without having you

07:30.250 --> 07:32.330
to build like subqueries and so on.

07:32.370 --> 07:35.050
You can go and use groupby together with the window functions.

07:35.090 --> 07:35.730
All right guys.

07:35.730 --> 07:38.810
So those are the four rules for the SQL window functions.
